Ride-hailing services have emerged as a transformative force in urban mobility, offering flexible transportation options that can complement or substitute public transit. However, their widespread adoption has also generated significant challenges, such as traffic congestion and increased pollution. This paper explores the potential of electric ride-hailing to improve urban mobility while addressing these negative externalities. We present a simulation model designed to assess the impact of electric ride-hailing on urban mobility and infrastructure. Our analysis reveals that while electric ride-hailing can lower overall emissions, it may exacerbate traffic congestion. Nevertheless, our results demonstrate that a substantial portion of cities could achieve zero-emission road networks through the implementation of dynamic pricing policies and infrastructure investments like charging stations. These insights provide valuable guidance for policymakers and urban planners regarding the integration of ride-hailing into urban mobility ecosystems.

