Membership inference attacks (MIAs) are a significant privacy threat, yet their relationship with data utility remains underexplored. In this paper, we examine the trade-off between data utility and MIA susceptibility in synthetic data. We find that the degree of privacy in the original data directly influences the susceptibility of the resulting synthetic data to MIAs. Specifically, we show that MIAs can be performed on synthetic data using a classifier trained on the original data, and that synthetic data derived from highly private sources are more vulnerable to these attacks. To validate these findings, we conducted experiments across multiple datasets and GAN architectures, including DCGAN, WGAN, and WGAN-GP. Our results indicate that the privacy level of the source data is a critical factor in determining the privacy of the synthetic output. Additionally, we observed that the structural correlations within synthetic data are a primary driver of MIA susceptibility. These insights provide valuable insights for developing synthetic data with enhanced privacy properties. Source code is available at https://github.com/ChengZhang1999/MIASD.

